@ id: dopd05
@ order: 624
@ title: How it maps to our fleet — endpoint vs direction, and self-improvement
@ subtitle: our distill.py copies the endpoint; Direct-OPD transfers the direction
@ source: fleet_agents/distill.py + harness_opt_gate.py + prompt-optimize triad

--- note
## Our distill.py IS endpoint distillation
The fleet's `distill.py` (the biohub cell-detection distiller) is a textbook **endpoint** distiller: a heavy
teacher (Cellpose-SAM / micro-SAM / StarDist3D) runs offline once to produce **pseudo-labels**, and a fast
one-pass 3D-UNet **student is trained to reproduce those labels**. The student is pulled to the teacher's
**endpoint** — exactly the mode Direct-OPD contrasts itself against. That is the *right* choice for us: our
teacher is **stronger** than the student (we distill to get its recall into a T4-fast net), so importing its
endpoint is the goal, not a hazard. Direct-OPD only wins when the teacher is **weaker** than the student and you
want its *direction*, not its level — the opposite regime.

--- note
## The one-line contrast
- **Endpoint distillation (our distill.py):** student → teacher's outputs. Ceiling = the teacher. Use when the
  teacher is the stronger model.
- **Direct-OPD (direction transfer):** student → moves along the teacher's RL shift Δ_T from its own base,
  on-policy, KL-leashed to itself. Ceiling = the student's own capacity. Use when the teacher is **weaker** but
  learned a useful *direction* via RL.

We encoded this decision as a small **pure** helper on `distill.py` — `direction_transfer_worth(teacher_pre,
teacher_post, student_ref)` — so the fleet can *decide* endpoint-vs-direction from measured numbers. It does
**not** implement RL (that needs verl/vLLM infra we don't carry); it is the planning gate, grounded in dopd06.

--- note
## The agentic self-improvement connection
Direct-OPD is one instance of a pattern the fleet already runs on its **prompt/harness** surfaces: **transfer
the improvement, gate on eval.**
- **`harness_opt_gate`** — our port of deepagents' *better-harness* keep-if-improves rule: a change to a
  PROMPT / HARNESS is accepted only if it beats the incumbent on a blind holdout. Same shape as Direct-OPD's
  KL-leashed, reward-gated step: move only where the signal is real; anchor to your prior self.
- **the prompt-optimize triad** (`prompt_metric` / `prompt_dataset` / `dspy_prompt_pack` + `prompt_agent_author`
  / `skill_build`) — self-evolving prompts: propose a shift, score it, keep it if it improves. That IS
  "replicate the shift, gate on eval," at the text level rather than the token-logit level.
- **`gm_repo_distill`** — the self-improving loop that scans a GM winner repo for techniques the fleet lacks;
  Direct-OPD's "do the expensive discovery once, transfer the direction" is the same economics.

--- note
## Honest framing (say it plainly)
Direct-OPD is **LLM-RL post-training infrastructure**, not a cell-tracking method and not a biohub lever. It
enters the hub as (a) background that sharpens how we think about our *own* distillation (endpoint vs
direction), and (b) a **candidate** capability for a future **LLM-RL competition**. What we actually added to the
fleet now is cheap and honest: the endpoint-vs-direction **decision helper** + its test (dopd06 / part 4). The
full Direct-OPD **trainer** (verl + vLLM rollouts + frozen teacher log-ratios) is **flagged, not built** — we
build it only when a real reward-aligned LLM-RL comp needs it, exactly as we flagged the NVFP4/AWQ trainers.
